INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-vine-and-grape', 'How grapes grow and ripen', 'Term', ARRAY['acidity', 'sweetness', 'alcohol']::text[], 'Every bottle of wine starts as a bunch of grapes, so it helps to know what a grape is made of. Cut one open and you find two main parts. The skin is the thin outer layer, and it holds the colour and the tannin. The pulp is the juicy flesh inside, and it is mostly water, sugar and acid. The sugar is what later becomes alcohol, and the acid is what keeps a wine tasting fresh.

Even in black grapes, the pulp and juice are almost always clear. The colour of a red wine comes from the skin of the black grape, not from its juice or pulp. That is why the way a winemaker handles the skins decides whether a wine ends up red, pink or white.

A vine follows the same cycle every year. After a winter rest it puts out buds and leaves in spring. Then the vine flowers. Flowering comes before the grapes form, because each grape grows from a small pollinated flower. Through the summer the tiny green berries swell, and later they change colour and ripen until harvest in autumn.

Ripening is a trade. As grapes ripen on the vine, their sugar goes up and their acid goes down. A grape picked early is sour and not very sweet; a grape left longer is sweeter and softer. Choosing the picking date is how a grower sets the balance between freshness and richness.

Climate changes how far that trade goes. In a cool climate, grapes ripen more slowly and less fully, so they keep more of their acid, and the wine tastes fresher and sharper. Nobody adds vinegar to get that crisp taste; it simply comes from grapes that did not lose much acid.

In a warm climate, warmth and sunshine let grapes ripen fully and build up more sugar. Yeast turns sugar into alcohol during fermentation, so more sugar in the grapes usually means more alcohol in the wine. That is why warm-climate wines often have more alcohol, even though they are not fortified and nothing extra has been added.

So when you taste a wine and feel it is very fresh, or notice a warm, rich feel, you are partly tasting the weather of the place where the grapes grew.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-making-wine', 'How red, white and rosé wines are made', 'Term', ARRAY['tannin', 'appearance', 'alcohol']::text[], 'Wine is made by fermentation. Alcoholic fermentation needs two things: sugar and yeast. Yeast are tiny living organisms. When they meet the sugar in grape juice, they eat it and turn it into alcohol and carbon dioxide gas.

So the alcohol in wine comes from yeast turning the sugar in grape juice into alcohol. It is not poured in after bottling, and it does not come from oak barrels or from the grape''s acid.

Besides alcohol, fermentation produces carbon dioxide. In a still wine this gas simply floats away. In a sparkling wine it is trapped, and that is where the bubbles come from.

For white wine, the grapes are crushed and then pressed before fermentation, so only the juice is fermented. The skins are taken away early, which keeps colour and tannin out of the wine. After fermentation the wine may be matured for a while and is then bottled.

Red wine follows a different order. The black grapes are crushed and fermented together with their skins. Then the new wine is drained off, and the skins are pressed to squeeze out the wine left in them. After that comes maturation and finally bottling. So the order for red wine is crushing, fermentation, draining, pressing, maturation, bottling.

This difference explains tannin. Tannin comes mainly from grape skins. Red wine has tannin because it ferments together with the skins, which contain tannin; white wine has very little because its juice was pressed off the skins before fermentation. It is not about extra sugar, extra yeast or how soon the wine is bottled.

Rosé sits in between. It is made from black grapes, but the juice touches the skins for only a short time before it is drained off. Less time on the skins means less colour, so the wine is pink rather than deep red. Most rosé is not red wine mixed with water, it is not white wine aged in old red-wine barrels, and it is not made from special pink grapes.

A well-known example is White Zinfandel. Despite the word white in its name, White Zinfandel is a pink, medium-sweet wine made from the black Zinfandel grape. It is usually light and easy to drink.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-wine-types', 'Still, sparkling and fortified wines', 'Term', ARRAY['sparkling', 'champagne', 'alcohol']::text[], 'Most wines you will meet fall into one of three types, and the difference is easy to feel in the glass.

A still wine has no bubbles and no added spirit. Most wine in the world is still wine, whether it is red, white or rosé, and whether it is dry or sweet. Dry is a word about sugar, not about bubbles, so it describes something else.

A sparkling wine has bubbles. In Champagne, Cava and Prosecco, the bubbles are carbon dioxide made by yeast during fermentation and trapped in the wine. The winemaker runs a second fermentation in a sealed bottle or a sealed tank, so the gas cannot escape and dissolves into the wine. No air is pumped in, no fizzy water is added, and the cork does not create the gas. When you open the bottle, the gas comes out as bubbles.

Three famous names are worth remembering. Champagne is a sparkling wine from the Champagne region of France. Cava is a sparkling wine from Spain. Prosecco is a sparkling wine from Italy. By contrast, Chablis is a dry white, Sauternes is a sweet white and Rioja is a red, so none of those are sparkling.

A fortified wine has grape spirit added to it. Port and Sherry are both examples of fortified wine. Port comes from Portugal and Sherry comes from Spain. Adding spirit raises the alcohol, which is why Port has more alcohol than most wines: grape spirit is added, bringing it to around 20 percent. Fermentation on its own does not reach that level, however long it lasts.

You can feel that difference. Alcohol gives a warming feeling at the back of the throat. A glass of Port gives a much stronger warming feeling than a Prosecco, a light off-dry German Riesling or a White Zinfandel, which all have moderate or low alcohol.

So when you pick up a glass, ask two quick questions. Are there bubbles? Is there that strong warmth of added spirit? If the answer to both is no, you are drinking a still wine.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
UPDATE articles SET slug = 'l1-art-of-tasting', category = 'Term', topics = ARRAY['tasting', 'palate', 'acidity', 'tannin']::text[], content = 'Tasting wine is mostly about noticing what happens in your mouth and giving it a name. Two of the most useful words are acidity and tannin, and each one has a clear feeling you can learn to spot.

Start with acidity. Take a sip and wait a moment. If your mouth starts to water, that is acidity. Acid makes you salivate in the same way lemon juice does, and the more your mouth waters after a sip, the higher the acidity.

People often describe high acidity as fresh, crisp or sharp. A wine that tastes fresh and sharp, like biting into a green apple, is showing acidity. Green apples and lemons are high in acid, which is why they are the usual comparison.

A wine with low acidity feels soft and round, and sometimes a bit flat. There is little to make your mouth water, so the wine can feel gentle. If there is very little acid, some people call it flabby. Either way, it is simply a description of what you feel.

Now tannin. Tannin creates a drying, astringent feeling in the mouth. It binds to proteins in your saliva, so your gums, teeth and tongue can feel dry and a little rough, like after strong black tea. Tannin is not sweetness, saltiness or fizz; it is a texture.

Tannin comes mainly from grape skins, so it is found mostly in red wines. Thick-skinned black grapes give the most. A young Cabernet Sauvignon is likely to leave your gums feeling strongly dry, because Cabernet Sauvignon has thick skins and high tannin. White wines such as Pinot Grigio, rosés such as White Zinfandel and sparkling wines such as Prosecco have very little tannin.

It helps to keep the feelings apart. Mouth-watering means acidity. Drying gums mean tannin. A warm feeling in the throat points to alcohol, and a heavy, mouth-filling feel points to body.

A good way to practise is to take one sip and ask one question at a time. Is my mouth watering? Are my gums drying? Say what you feel, then put the word to it. Whatever you notice is a useful start.' WHERE title = 'The Art of Wine Tasting' AND (slug IS NULL OR slug = 'l1-art-of-tasting');
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-body-alcohol-sweetness', 'Body, alcohol and sweetness', 'Term', ARRAY['body', 'alcohol', 'sweetness', 'palate']::text[], 'Three more words help you describe how a wine feels: body, alcohol and sweetness.

Body is a wine''s perceived weight on the palate. It is how heavy or light the wine feels in your mouth. A helpful comparison is milk. A wine that feels like water or skimmed milk is light-bodied. A wine that feels heavy and mouth-filling, like whole milk, is full-bodied. Body is a different thing from acidity, from the finish (how long the flavour lasts) and from the bouquet (the smell).

Several things add to body, but alcohol is one of the biggest. More alcohol usually makes a wine feel fuller in body, because alcohol adds weight and richness in the mouth. Higher acidity, more bubbles or serving a wine colder do not make it fuller. This is one reason warm-climate wines, which tend to have more alcohol, are often full-bodied.

You can also feel alcohol directly. A warming or burning sensation at the back of your throat after swallowing points to high alcohol. The stronger the warmth, the higher the alcohol. Acidity, by contrast, makes your mouth water, and tannin dries your gums.

Sweetness is about sugar you can taste. Most wines are fermented until almost all the sugar has become alcohol, and these are called dry. When some sugar is left in the wine, you feel it on your tongue. A wine that tastes of honey and leaves a sugary, slightly sticky feel on your tongue is sweet.

A famous example is Sauternes. Sauternes, from Bordeaux, is a sweet white wine with rich, honeyed flavours. It is one of the best-known sweet wines in the world.

Between dry and sweet there are steps, such as off-dry and medium-sweet. White Zinfandel, for example, is usually medium-sweet. To decide, pay attention to your tongue and to that slightly sticky feel, rather than to the smell.

Try putting all three together in one sentence: how heavy the wine feels, how warm it is, and how much sugar you can taste. That sentence already says a lot about the wine.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
UPDATE articles SET slug = 'l1-naming-smells', category = 'Term', topics = ARRAY['aroma', 'nose', 'tasting']::text[], content = 'When you smell a wine, it helps to start with the family of the aroma rather than hunting for the perfect word. Fruit is the biggest family. Others include flowers, green and herbal notes like grass, and the warm, sweet-spice smells that come from oak.

Fruit smells change with the grape and the place. Green fruit such as green apple and pear, citrus such as lemon, stone fruit such as peach, tropical fruit such as pineapple, red fruit such as strawberry and cherry, and black fruit such as blackcurrant and plum are all useful words. Whatever you notice is a real observation, and there is no single correct answer.

Climate has a big effect. One Chardonnay can taste of green apple while another tastes of pineapple because of climate: cool regions give green fruit, warm regions give riper tropical fruit. In a cool place like Chablis, Chardonnay tastes of green apple and citrus. In a warm place the grapes get riper, and the same grape gives peach and tropical fruit. Both are still Chardonnay; nothing has been added, and the cork has nothing to do with it.

Some aromas come from the winemaking rather than the grape. Vanilla and toast aromas usually come from oak barrels. Wines fermented or aged in oak can also pick up smoke, coconut or sweet spice. Wines made in stainless steel tanks pick up none of these, so their fruit stays clean and fresh.

One common trap is mixing up smell and taste. A wine can smell of very ripe fruit and still be dry. Fruity smells are not the same as sugar. Sweetness is about sugar you can taste on your tongue, so if a wine smells of ripe fruit but you cannot taste any sugar in it, the wine is dry.

A simple routine works well. Smell the wine before swirling and again after. Say the family first: fruit, flowers, green, or oak. Then, if a more exact word comes to mind, add it. If nothing comes, say what the smell reminds you of, or what it is not. That is still a tasting note.' WHERE title = 'Naming what you smell' AND (slug IS NULL OR slug = 'l1-naming-smells');
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-white-grapes', 'The main white grapes', 'Term', ARRAY['riesling', 'burgundy', 'acidity', 'aroma']::text[], 'A handful of white grapes make a large share of the white wines you will see. Learning their usual style helps you guess what is in the glass.

First, a quick check on colour. Riesling, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Grigio are white grapes. Merlot, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon and Pinot Noir are black grapes, used for red and rosé wines.

Chardonnay is very flexible. It can be light and crisp or rich and full, and it can be made with or without oak. Chablis, in the north of Burgundy in France, is made entirely from Chardonnay. The cool climate there gives it high acidity and flavours of green apple and citrus.

Sauvignon Blanc is known for fresh, green aromas: gooseberry, green apple, grass and herbs. It is usually made without oak so that these lively smells stay fresh, so vanilla, toast, blackcurrant or toffee are not what people expect from it.

Sancerre, from France''s Loire Valley, is a dry white wine made from Sauvignon Blanc. French labels often name the place rather than the grape, so it is worth learning which grape goes with which name.

Riesling wines are known for high acidity and floral, fruity aromas. Riesling is an aromatic white grape, and its wines smell of flowers, citrus and stone fruit such as peach. They are rarely oaky and have no tannin to speak of. Riesling can be made dry or sweet, and German examples are often light and lower in alcohol.

Pinot Grigio is most often made as a light-bodied, dry white with delicate fruit. Most Pinot Grigio, especially from Italy, is refreshing, with gentle citrus and green-apple flavours rather than strong aromas. It is not usually sweet or full-bodied.

When you taste a white wine, try matching what you feel to these patterns. Very fresh with flowers and citrus might be Riesling. Green and grassy might be Sauvignon Blanc. Light, dry and gentle might be Pinot Grigio. Green apple with crisp acidity might be a cool-climate Chardonnay like Chablis. These are starting points, not rules, and plenty of wines surprise even experienced tasters.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-black-grapes', 'The main black grapes', 'Term', ARRAY['cabernet', 'merlot', 'pinot', 'bordeaux', 'tannin']::text[], 'Black grapes give red and rosé wines. A few of them are grown all over the world, and each has a recognisable style.

Cabernet Sauvignon has thick skins, so its wines are deep in colour and high in tannin. Black fruit is its signature, and blackcurrant is the aroma most typical of Cabernet Sauvignon. Lemon, peach and green apple belong to white wines, not to Cabernet. Oak-aged examples can also smell of cedar or vanilla.

Merlot is the softer partner. Merlot is a black grape known for soft, round, plummy wines, with plum and red-fruit flavours. It is widely grown in Bordeaux, where it is one of the two main grapes.

That is why red Bordeaux is usually a blend of grapes such as Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot. The firm, tannic Cabernet and the soft, plummy Merlot each bring something the other lacks. Red Bordeaux is not made from Pinot Noir or Syrah on their own, and it does not mix in white grapes.

Pinot Noir is the red grape of Burgundy, not Bordeaux. It has thin skins. Compared with Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir usually has lower tannin and a paler colour, with red-fruit flavours such as cherry and raspberry. It is not sweeter or fizzier than Cabernet; it is simply lighter and softer in structure.

Syrah and Shiraz are two names for the same black grape. France uses the name Syrah, while Australia made the name Shiraz famous. Black fruit and black pepper are the typical flavours of Syrah or Shiraz. Its wines are usually deep in colour, with blackberry and plum fruit and a peppery, spicy note.

A simple way to remember them is by weight and fruit. Pinot Noir is pale and light with red fruit. Merlot is soft and plummy. Cabernet Sauvignon is dark and firm with blackcurrant. Syrah is dark with black fruit and pepper. When you taste a red, ask how dark it looks, how much your gums dry, and whether the fruit seems red or black.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-red-wine-places', 'Famous red wine names', 'Region', ARRAY['body', 'tannin', 'label']::text[], 'Many European wines are named after the place they come from, not the grape. Learning a few of these names helps you know what to expect before you even open the bottle.

Rioja is the most famous red wine region of Spain. Its reds are often aged in oak, which can add vanilla notes. If you see Rioja on a label, think of Spain. For comparison, Chianti is a red from Italy, Beaujolais is a red from France and Sancerre is a French white wine.

Châteauneuf-du-Pape is a red wine from the Rhône Valley in France. It comes from the southern Rhône, the same area as Côtes du Rhône. The climate there is warm, so these are full-bodied reds with plenty of alcohol and ripe fruit. It is not from Burgundy, Bordeaux or Champagne.

Beaujolais is at the other end of the scale. A typical Beaujolais is a light, fruity red with low tannin, full of juicy red-berry fruit. It comes from just south of Burgundy in France and is made from the Gamay grape. Many people serve it lightly chilled to bring out its freshness.

So if you are asked which red wine is most likely to feel light-bodied, Beaujolais is the safest choice. Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Australian Shiraz and Port are all fuller wines, with more alcohol and more weight in the mouth. Port is even fortified, with grape spirit added.

It helps to picture these wines on a line from light to full. Beaujolais sits at the light end: pale, juicy, soft. Rioja usually sits in the middle, though some are richer. Châteauneuf-du-Pape and Australian Shiraz sit near the full end, warm and mouth-filling. Port sits beyond them, with its extra alcohol.

You do not have to remember every place on a map. Start with these few names and the feeling that goes with each. When you taste one, notice its weight and its tannin, and see whether your experience matches the name.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-storing-wine', 'Storing wine and keeping an open bottle fresh', 'Term', ARRAY['service']::text[], 'How you keep wine affects how it tastes. The good news is that the rules are simple.

Heat, light and big swings in temperature all damage wine. The best place for storing wine is a cool, dark place with a steady temperature. A sunny windowsill, a shelf next to the oven or a garage that gets very hot in summer are all poor choices, because heat and light make the wine age badly and lose its fresh fruit.

The way you lay a bottle down matters too. Bottles closed with a cork should be stored lying on their side. This keeps the wine touching the cork, which keeps the cork moist. A cork that dries out can shrink and let air in, and oxygen spoils wine. Lying a bottle down does not make the wine age faster, keep bubbles in or protect the label. Bottles with screw caps do not need this, though lying them down does no harm.

Once a bottle is open, air becomes the main enemy. Oxygen in the air slowly makes opened wine taste stale and flat, losing its fresh fruit. Putting the cork or cap back and keeping the bottle in the fridge already helps. There are also two common tools that slow the process down.

A vacuum pump removes air from the bottle. You fit a rubber stopper and pump the air out, so less oxygen is left to touch the wine. It does not add sugar, chill the wine or add bubbles. This can keep a wine fresh for a few more days.

A blanket system works a different way. It lays a protective gas over the wine so air cannot reach it. The gas is harmless and heavier than air, so it sits on the surface of the wine like a blanket and keeps oxygen away. It does not wrap or freeze the bottle, and it does not add spirit.

Whichever method you use, the idea is the same: keep wine cool, dark and away from oxygen, and it will keep its flavour for longer.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-serving-wine', 'Serving wine: temperature and opening', 'Term', ARRAY['service', 'sparkling', 'body']::text[], 'Serving temperature changes how a wine tastes, and the rule follows the style of the wine.

Sparkling wines, including Champagne, should be served well chilled. Cold keeps the bubbles lively and the wine refreshing, and it also makes the bottle safer to open. Light-bodied and medium-bodied white and rosé wines, and sweet wines, are also served well chilled.

Full-bodied whites, such as an oaked Chardonnay, are served lightly chilled. If they are too cold, their rich flavours are muted; if they are too warm, they taste heavy.

Light-bodied reds with low tannin, such as Beaujolais, can be served lightly chilled. A little cool brings out their fresh, fruity side. They should not be served as cold as a sparkling wine, warmed up or poured over ice.

Medium-bodied and full-bodied reds, such as Bordeaux, are served at room temperature. If they are too cold, the tannins taste harsher and the fruit is hidden. Warming a wine above room temperature does not help either.

Opening a sparkling wine needs care, because the cork is under high pressure and can fly out. The safe way is to hold the cork, turn the bottle slowly, and point it away from people. Keep a hand or a cloth over the cork once the wire cage is loosened. Never shake the bottle first, and do not pull the cork out fast for a loud pop. The aim is a gentle sigh, not a bang, which also keeps more of the bubbles in the wine.

Before you pour for a guest, take a quick look at the wine and smell it. You look at and smell a wine before serving it to check that it is clean and not faulty. A sound wine looks clear and smells fresh. If it smells musty, like wet cardboard, or seems stale, it may be faulty, and it is better to find that out before your guest tastes it. This check does not warm the wine, sweeten it or release bubbles.

Getting these small steps right lets the wine show at its best, and it makes serving feel relaxed rather than tricky.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l1-food-and-wine', 'Food and wine: what changes in the glass', 'Term', ARRAY['pairing']::text[], 'Food changes how a wine tastes. The same glass can seem softer or harsher depending on what you eat with it. A few simple effects explain most of what happens. None of this decides what you should like; it simply helps you understand what you notice.

Sweet food tends to make a dry wine taste more acidic and bitter, and less fruity. Sugar in the food makes the wine seem sharper and thinner by comparison. That is why a wine served with dessert should be at least as sweet as the food.

Salty food works the other way. Salty food tends to make a wine taste less bitter and less acidic. Salt makes tannin seem softer and acid less sharp, and it can make the wine feel fuller. Salt is one of the most wine-friendly tastes you can find in food, which is why salty cheese and snacks often go well with many wines.

Acidity in food has a softening effect too. Food with high acidity, such as a lemony salad dressing, tends to make a wine taste less acidic and more fruity. The acid in the food balances the acid in the wine. A wine with high acidity usually works best with acidic food, while a low-acid wine can taste flat next to it.

The strength of flavour matters as well. When a light, delicate wine is served with a strongly flavoured dish, the food usually overpowers the flavours of the wine. The wine does not become sweeter or turn sparkling; it simply gets lost. So it helps to match intensity: strongly flavoured food needs a wine with more flavour, and gentle dishes suit gentler wines.

A good way to learn is to try it yourself. Take a sip of wine, then a bite of food, then sip again. Ask what changed. Did the wine taste sharper or softer? More bitter or more fruity? Did its flavour fade or stay? Your answers are the real lesson, and everyone''s sensitivity is a little different.

Over time you will start to predict the changes before you taste, and choosing a wine for a meal becomes much easier.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
UPDATE articles SET slug = 'l2-bordeaux', category = 'Region', topics = ARRAY['bordeaux', 'cabernet', 'merlot', 'label']::text[], content = 'Bordeaux is a blend region shaped by two banks of the Gironde estuary in south-west France. Most of its red wine is a mix of grapes, and which grape leads the blend depends largely on which bank the vineyard sits on.

The Left Bank, including the Médoc and the Haut-Médoc, sits on warm, free-draining gravel. Pauillac and Margaux are in the Haut-Médoc, and their reds are blends led by Cabernet Sauvignon. Cabernet ripens late, and the gravel holds warmth that helps it get there. The wines are dark, firm and built to age.

The Right Bank sits on cooler clay and limestone. Saint-Émilion and Pomerol make reds led by Merlot, which ripens earlier and suits those cooler soils. These wines are rounder and often ready sooner.

Why blend at all? Cabernet Sauvignon brings structure: tannin, acidity and black fruit. Merlot adds soft, plummy fruit and body that round out Cabernet''s firm tannin. It is not there to add sweetness or bubbles, and it does not remove tannin; it simply fills the gaps, so the blend is more balanced than either grape alone. Cabernet Franc often plays a smaller supporting role.

Bordeaux also makes white wine. The dry whites of Graves and Pessac-Léognan usually blend Sauvignon Blanc with Sémillon, and the best are fermented and aged in oak. Sémillon is also the main grape of sweet Sauternes.

Reading a Bordeaux label takes practice, because several names sit together. Take a bottle from Château Marquis d''Alesme. The château is the producer. The word set underneath is the appellation, the place. On that label, Margaux is the village under the château name, so Margaux is the appellation, not Pauillac, Pomerol or Saint-Émilion. Village names such as Margaux, Pauillac, Saint-Julien and Saint-Estèphe are places, not grapes.

Rankings are printed too. "Grand Cru Classé" on a Médoc label means the estate is on the 1855 classification; it is a rank, not a place. Cru Bourgeois is a classification for Médoc châteaux that are not in the 1855 list. It belongs to the Médoc alone: Saint-Émilion, on the Right Bank, has its own separate classification, and Pomerol and Sauternes do not use the Cru Bourgeois name.' WHERE title = 'Understanding Bordeaux' AND (slug IS NULL OR slug = 'l2-bordeaux');
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-vineyard-climate', 'Where grapes ripen: climate, slope and altitude', 'Term', ARRAY['acidity', 'quality', 'cabernet']::text[], 'Every wine starts with a grape that has to ripen, and ripening depends on warmth and sunlight. Over the summer the grape fills with sugar while its sharp acidity slowly falls. Where a vineyard sits decides how far that process goes.

The vine''s year has a few milestones. In spring it buds, then flowers; in summer the small, hard green berries swell. Véraison is the point when grapes change colour and begin to soften. Black grapes turn from green to red-purple, white grapes turn more translucent, and sugar starts to build up. Véraison is not flowering, not picking day and not winter pruning; it is the start of ripening. Harvest comes weeks later, when the grower judges that sugar, acid and flavour are in balance.

In a cool region, every bit of sun counts. In the Northern Hemisphere, south-facing slopes get more sunlight, which helps the grapes ripen. The sun sits to the south, so a slope tilted towards it catches more direct light and warmth. It does not keep rain off, and the soil holds no extra sugar; the gain is simply sunshine. In a marginal region that extra warmth can decide whether a vintage ripens fully. In the Southern Hemisphere the same logic points to north-facing slopes.

Altitude works the other way. Planting at high altitude makes the vineyard cooler, because temperature drops as you climb. Growers in hot places, such as parts of Argentina, plant high to slow ripening and keep fresh acidity in the grapes. High vineyards still need sunlight, and altitude does not bring tropical rain.

Grape varieties have their own timetables. Some ripen early and suit cool places. Others are late. Cabernet Sauvignon ripens late and needs plenty of warmth to ripen fully, so it is mostly grown in moderate or warm climates. It is not an early ripener at risk of overripening, it does not need frost, and its skins are thick rather than thin. Grown somewhere too cool, it can taste green and herbaceous, like green bell pepper.

Cool climates in general give wines with higher acidity, lighter body and fresher fruit. Warm climates give riper fruit, more sugar, and so more alcohol and body. Linking a wine''s style to its climate is one of the most useful habits a taster can build.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-quality-categories', 'Quality categories on European labels', 'Term', ARRAY['label', 'quality']::text[], 'European wine law sorts wines into two broad quality tiers, and each country prints its own name for them. Learning the names makes a label far easier to read.

The higher tier is PDO, Protected Designation of Origin. Compared with a PGI wine, a PDO wine has stricter rules on area, grape varieties and winemaking. A PDO comes from a smaller, tightly defined area, and its rules may set which grapes are allowed, maximum yields and how the wine is made. PDO says nothing about sweetness; a PDO wine can be dry or sweet.

The broader tier is PGI, Protected Geographical Indication. PGI areas are larger and the rules looser, so producers have more freedom over grapes and methods. That freedom is why many PGI wines are labelled by grape variety. Looser does not mean no rules at all: a PGI wine must still come from its named area.

In France, PDO wines are labelled AOC or AOP. IGP is the French term for PGI. A good example is Pays d''Oc, a large IGP covering the Languedoc-Roussillon in southern France. A bottle saying Pays d''Oc is an IGP wine from southern France; it is not a Burgundy Grand Cru, not a sparkling term and not a Bordeaux address.

Italy has two PDO levels. DOCG is Italy''s top quality category, sitting above DOC. IGT is Italy''s PGI level. Spain also has two PDO levels: DO, and above it DOCa, its top category. Rioja carries the DOCa category, one level above DO. Vino de la Tierra is Spain''s PGI level. Remember that DOCG is Italian and DO is Spanish, so neither belongs on a French label, and IGP is not a Spanish quality level above DO.

Germany grades its top wines differently, by how ripe the grapes were at harvest; its Prädikatswein system is explained in the Riesling article.

Some words on a label are not legal categories at all. Vieilles Vignes means old vines. Older vines tend to give smaller crops of more concentrated grapes, but there is no fixed legal age, so the term is a producer''s claim rather than a guarantee. It does not mean late harvest, noble rot or bottled at the estate.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-winemaking', 'Winemaking choices: skins, oak, lees and blending', 'Term', ARRAY['tannin', 'acidity', 'orange', 'body']::text[], 'Two wines from the same grapes can taste very different because of choices made in the winery. A handful of decisions explain most of those differences.

Start with the skins. Colour and tannin come from grape skins, not the juice, which is almost clear. To make a red with more colour and tannin, a winemaker increases extraction, for example by keeping the wine on its skins for longer. Warmer fermentation and more frequent mixing of skins and juice also pull out more. Fermenting juice without skins, adding sugar or bottling early would not help.

The same idea explains orange wine. Orange wine is made by fermenting white grapes with their skins, the way red wine is made. The skin contact gives an amber colour and some grip. No orange peel is involved, and it is not a blend of red and white or an oxidised rosé.

Next, the vessel. Stainless steel is neutral and easy to keep cool, so a winemaker ferments a white in steel to keep fresh, fruity flavours with no oak influence. That suits Sauvignon Blanc and Riesling. Steel adds no vanilla, toast, tannin or sweetness.

Oak is the alternative. New, small oak barrels give more oak flavour, such as vanilla and toast. New oak still holds its flavour compounds, and a small barrel puts more wood in contact with each litre of wine. Old or very large barrels add little oak flavour, and oak does not add acidity.

Malolactic conversion is a change carried out by bacteria after fermentation. It softens acidity by turning sharp malic acid into softer lactic acid. Malic acid tastes like green apple; lactic acid is milder, and the wine can pick up buttery notes. It does not add tannin, alcohol or bubbles. Most reds go through it; many crisp whites do not.

Lees are the dead yeast cells left after fermentation. Leaving a white wine in contact with its lees can add bread or biscuit flavours and a creamier texture. It adds no tannin, colour, bubbles or black fruit.

Finally, blending. Large brands blend wines from many vineyards and regions to make a consistent style every year. Mixing sites and vintages smooths out differences, so each bottle tastes the way loyal customers expect. Blending is a choice, not a legal requirement, and it does not remove alcohol.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-sweet-wines', 'How sweet wines are made', 'Term', ARRAY['sweetness', 'acidity', 'alcohol']::text[], 'Sweetness in wine is grape sugar that the yeast did not turn into alcohol. Winemakers have several ways to keep it there.

The simplest is to stop the fermentation before all the sugar has turned into alcohol. Chilling the wine and removing the yeast, for example, leaves natural grape sugar behind. Fermenting for longer would make the wine drier, while new oak or carbon dioxide add no sweetness. Sweetness can also be added after fermentation, and fortified wines such as Port stop fermentation by adding spirit.

The finest sweet wines start with concentrated grapes. If the water in a grape is reduced, the juice that is left is richer in sugar, acid and flavour.

Noble rot is welcome because it draws water out of the grapes, concentrating their sugar and acid for sweet wine. It is a fungus called botrytis that, with misty autumn mornings followed by dry afternoons, shrivels ripe grapes. It adds no tannin, gives no frost protection and does not turn white grapes red.

Sauternes, in Bordeaux, is the classic example. The main grape in Sauternes is Sémillon, often with some Sauvignon Blanc. Sémillon''s thin skins make it prone to noble rot. In Hungary, Tokaji Aszú is made mainly from Furmint grapes shrivelled by noble rot. Aszú is the local word for these botrytised grapes. German Trockenbeerenauslese uses the same rot on Riesling.

Icewine, or Eiswein, takes a colder route. The grapes are picked and pressed while frozen on the vine. Much of the water stays behind as ice, so only a little very sweet, concentrated juice runs out. Canada and Germany are famous for it. Icewine grapes are not dried on straw mats, picked early while green, or simply fermented in cold steel tanks.

Drying grapes after harvest is a third route. For Amarone della Valpolicella, the grapes are partly dried before fermentation, concentrating their sugar. Corvina grapes rest for months and lose water. Amarone is then usually fermented until dry, so the extra sugar becomes extra alcohol and body: the wine is full-bodied and high in alcohol rather than sweet. No grape spirit, frozen grapes or sugar syrup are involved.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-ageing-storage', 'Bottle ageing, storage and wine faults', 'Term', ARRAY['tannin', 'appearance', 'service', 'quality']::text[], 'Most wine is made to drink young. A smaller group, usually reds with plenty of tannin and acidity, can improve for years in bottle. Knowing what age does, and what damage smells like, helps you judge what is in your glass.

Colour is the first clue. As a red wine ages in bottle, it becomes paler and turns from purple towards garnet and brown. Young reds are purple at the rim; over the years the colour fades through garnet to tawny. An old red does not turn deeper purple or pink, and it does not stay the same. Whites go the other way and deepen towards gold.

Texture changes too. With bottle age, the tannins in a red wine become softer. Some tannin links up with colour pigments and drops out as sediment, so a tough young red can become smooth. Tannins do not get harsher, and they do not turn into acid or sugar.

That sediment is why you might decant an old red: to separate the wine from its sediment. Stand the bottle upright for a day, then pour slowly into a decanter, stopping when the deposit reaches the neck. Vintage Port needs the same care. Decanting does not sweeten, chill or add bubbles.

Good storage keeps wine ageing slowly. Keep bottles somewhere cool, dark and steady in temperature, away from vibration, and lay cork-sealed bottles on their sides so the cork stays moist.

Two faults are worth knowing by smell. Cork taint makes a wine smell musty, like damp cardboard or a wet cellar. It comes from a chemical called TCA that can be present in cork, and it flattens the fruit. It is not caused by heat, too much oak or noble rot. If you suspect it, compare with another bottle of the same wine.

Heat is the other enemy. A wine damaged by heat often tastes cooked or stewed, with jammy fruit. Heat speeds up ageing and cooks the fruit, so the wine loses freshness. A warning sign is a cork pushed part-way out of the bottle, or sticky wine around the capsule. Heat damage is not musty like cork taint, and it does not make a wine fizzy or crisp.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-service-pairing', 'Serving temperature and food pairing', 'Term', ARRAY['service', 'pairing', 'sweetness', 'alcohol', 'acidity']::text[], 'Serving a wine well takes two decisions: how cold to pour it and what to eat with it.

Temperature first. Light- and medium-bodied whites, such as Sancerre, are served chilled. Chilling keeps them crisp and refreshing; at room temperature they feel flat and the alcohol stands out. Rosés are chilled too, and sparkling and sweet wines are served well chilled. Light reds such as Beaujolais can take a light chill, while medium- and full-bodied reds are best at room temperature, where their tannin feels softer. Full-bodied, oaked whites can be served a little less cold so their flavours open up.

Food changes how a wine tastes, and a few rules cover most meals. Think about what the food does to the wine.

Sweetness in food is the biggest challenge. Sweet food makes a dry wine taste sour and thin. For a very sweet dessert, the best match is a wine that is at least as sweet as the dessert. A bone-dry, tannic red, a light dry white or a Brut sparkling wine will all taste harsh next to it. A Sauternes or a sweet Muscat keeps its fruit and balance.

Umami is the savoury taste in mushrooms, aged cheese, cured meat and soy sauce. Umami-rich food tends to make a wine taste more bitter and acidic, and less fruity. Tannin and oak can seem harsher, while fruit and sweetness fade. Wines with soft tannin and ripe fruit cope better, and a little salt in the dish helps.

Chilli heat is the third. Chilli heat makes a wine''s alcohol feel more burning. Heat and alcohol add to each other, so a strong, high-alcohol red can feel fiery with a spicy curry; the alcohol does not become less noticeable or lower. Lower-alcohol wines, often with a touch of sweetness, such as an off-dry Riesling, are kinder partners.

Two tastes in food help wine instead. Salt makes a wine feel softer and fruitier. Acidity in food makes a wine seem less sharp, so a high-acid wine suits high-acid dishes. Fatty or rich food is matched well by a wine with good acidity or tannin, which cuts through it.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-burgundy-chardonnay', 'Burgundy, Chardonnay and Beaujolais', 'Region', ARRAY['burgundy', 'label', 'quality']::text[], 'Burgundy, in eastern France, makes its great wines from just two grapes. Red Burgundy is Pinot Noir and white Burgundy is Chardonnay. Because the grape rarely appears on the label, Burgundy labels name places instead, and the smaller the place, the higher the rank.

Burgundy ranks its vineyards in four steps. Regional wines say Bourgogne. Village wines carry a village name. Premier Cru vineyards are a step up. Grand Cru is the term for the very best vineyards, at the top of the ladder. So a Grand Cru outranks a Premier Cru, even though "premier" means first.

White Burgundy shows how much climate and winemaking shape Chardonnay. Puligny-Montrachet and Meursault are white Burgundies made from Chardonnay. They are top villages in the Côte de Beaune, and many of their wines are fermented and matured in oak, which gives a fuller, rounder style with notes of toast and nuts. They are not Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling or Pinot Gris.

Chablis sits far to the north, where it is cooler. Compared with a Meursault, a typical Chablis is leaner, with higher acidity and less obvious oak. Both are Chardonnay; Chablis is simply cooler and often unoaked, with green apple, citrus and a stony feel. It is not fuller, sweeter or lower in acid.

Chardonnay travels well, and warmer places push it further. A Napa Valley Chardonnay is the one most likely to be full-bodied with ripe tropical fruit and oak flavours. Napa, in California, is warm, so the grapes ripen fully, and many producers use new oak. A Mosel Riesling and a Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc are different grapes altogether, and Chablis is the lean, cool end of Chardonnay.

At the southern end of greater Burgundy lies Beaujolais. Beaujolais is made from the black grape Gamay, not Pinot Noir, Grenache or Merlot. Gamay gives light, juicy reds with red-berry fruit and low tannin, and they are good served lightly chilled. The best villages, such as Fleurie and Morgon, are labelled as Beaujolais Crus.

When you see a Burgundy label, look for the place name and its rank. The grape is almost always Pinot Noir for reds and Chardonnay for whites.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
UPDATE articles SET slug = 'l2-pinot-noir-label', category = 'Term', topics = ARRAY['label', 'pinot', 'burgundy']::text[], content = 'The largest words on a label are not always the place. On this bottle the producer is Nelson Hill. Pinot Noir, the grape, is written in script underneath. Anderson Valley is the appellation, a cool stretch of Mendocino County, California. The year 2013 is the vintage: the year those grapes were picked.

Read a label as four separate facts. Who made it. Which grape. Where it grew. Which year. The drawing of a vat is a trademark. It is not one of those four facts. On this label the grape named is Pinot Noir; Anderson Valley is the place, not the grape. Nothing on it says Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot or Sangiovese.

Knowing the grape tells you a lot about what is in the bottle. Pinot Noir is the grape of red Burgundy, which is made almost entirely from it. Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot belong to Bordeaux, and Syrah to the Rhône.

Pinot Noir has thin skins, so its wines are usually paler in colour with low to medium tannin. Colour and tannin both come from the skins, and Pinot has little of either. Expect a translucent ruby wine with a silky texture, not a deep, inky, heavily tannic one. Pinot Noir is normally dry; thin skins do not make it sweet.

Young Pinot Noir smells of red fruit such as strawberry, raspberry and cherry. As a good Pinot Noir ages in bottle, it can develop earthy and mushroom notes, along with forest floor and savoury, gamey hints. Pineapple and banana, fresh lime and grass, or pear drops are not part of its usual picture.

Pinot Noir is fussy about climate. It needs a long, gentle, fairly cool growing season, and in hot places it loses its perfume. That is why it does well in cool spots such as Burgundy, Oregon and California''s Anderson Valley. In New Zealand, Central Otago and Martinborough are best known for Pinot Noir. Both are cool enough to suit it and make some of the country''s finest examples, rather than Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah or Merlot.

So when you spot Pinot Noir on a label, expect a pale, fragrant, silky red.' WHERE title = 'Reading a Pinot Noir label' AND (slug IS NULL OR slug = 'l2-pinot-noir-label');
UPDATE articles SET slug = 'l2-tuscany-sangiovese', category = 'Region', topics = ARRAY['sangiovese', 'tuscany', 'label', 'acidity', 'tannin']::text[], content = 'Sangiovese is the red grape of Tuscany, in central Italy. It runs high in acid, with sour cherry, dried herbs, and a savory edge, rather than sweet jam. Tannin is medium to high, so the wines feel firm and fresh rather than soft.

Chianti Classico comes from Tuscany, from the hill country between Florence and Siena. It is not from Piedmont, the Veneto or Sicily. The black rooster on the neck marks that zone, and the wine has to be mostly Sangiovese. Plain Chianti comes from a wider area around it and is usually lighter and simpler.

Italian labels add words about ageing. Riserva usually means the wine was aged for longer before release. A Chianti Classico Riserva must meet a longer minimum ageing period than the standard version, often including time in oak barrels. It says nothing about sweetness, bubbles or white grapes; a Riserva is still a dry red. Riserva wines are often made from the best grapes of the year, so they tend to be more concentrated as well as older.

Further south, the same grape grown ripe around the hill town of Montalcino becomes Brunello. Brunello di Montalcino is made from Sangiovese, the same grape as Chianti. It is not Nebbiolo, which belongs to Piedmont, nor Montepulciano or Corvina. Brunello is darker, fuller and more tannic than most Chianti, and the rules require it to be aged for longer before release. It is meant to wait.

Quality labels follow the same pattern across Italy. Chianti Classico and Brunello di Montalcino both hold DOCG status, Italy''s highest category. Many Tuscan producers also make IGT wines, which have looser rules and sometimes blend Sangiovese with Cabernet Sauvignon or Merlot.

With age, Sangiovese''s bright cherry turns to dried cherry, leather and earth, and its tannins soften. A glass that feels sharp and savory, not soft and sweet, is Sangiovese doing what it does. That acidity is also why it works so well with tomato sauces and grilled meat.' WHERE title = 'Tuscany & Sangiovese' AND (slug IS NULL OR slug = 'l2-tuscany-sangiovese');
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-italy-regional', 'Italy beyond Tuscany: Nebbiolo, Pinot Grigio and more', 'Region', ARRAY['label', 'tannin', 'acidity']::text[], 'Italy grows hundreds of local grapes. Beyond Tuscany''s Sangiovese, a few names come up again and again.

In Piedmont, in the north-west, the great red grape is Nebbiolo. Barolo and Barbaresco are made entirely from Nebbiolo. They are not Sangiovese, Barbera or Corvina. Nebbiolo surprises people, because its wines are typically fairly pale in colour but high in both tannin and acidity. A Barolo can look light, garnet and almost delicate, then grip your gums hard. It is not deep purple with soft tannin, nor sweet and low in acid. Its aromas run to roses, red cherry and tar, and good Barolo needs years to soften.

Piedmont also makes whites. Gavi, from Piedmont, is made from the white grape Cortese. It is a light, dry white with high acidity and delicate citrus and green-apple fruit. Garganega is the grape of Soave in the Veneto, Verdicchio comes from central Italy and Fiano from the south, so none of those make Gavi. Piedmont''s Moscato grape makes sweet sparkling Asti, covered in the sparkling article.

The north-east is Pinot Grigio country. Friuli-Venezia Giulia and Delle Venezie are both in north-east Italy. Delle Venezie covers a large area and produces much of the world''s light, dry, crisp Pinot Grigio, while Friuli makes some more concentrated examples. Neither is in Tuscany, Sicily or the far south. The Veneto, also in the north-east, is home to Valpolicella and its rich, dried-grape Amarone, made from Corvina.

The far south has its own ripe reds. Puglia, the heel of Italy''s boot, grows Primitivo. Zinfandel in California and Primitivo in Puglia are the same grape variety, known by different names in the USA and southern Italy. Genetic tests confirmed the link. They are not white grapes, sparkling styles or types of oak barrel. In both places warm sun gives ripe, full-bodied reds with jammy black fruit and high alcohol.

Most of these wines carry Italy''s PDO labels. Barolo, Barbaresco and Gavi are DOCG, the top category, while many Pinot Grigio wines are DOC or IGT. Learn the grape behind each place name and Italian labels become much less mysterious.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-riesling-germany', 'Riesling and German wine labels', 'Region', ARRAY['riesling', 'label', 'sweetness', 'acidity']::text[], 'Riesling is a white grape famous for high acidity and intense aromas of citrus, green apple, peach and flowers. It is grown around the world, but Germany is its home.

Germany''s best-known Riesling regions follow its rivers. The Mosel is cool and steep. A Riesling from the Mosel is typically light-bodied with high acidity, often low in alcohol and sometimes off-dry. Many keep a little natural sweetness, which balances the racy acid. It is never full-bodied and oaky, red or fortified. The Rheingau and the Pfalz are wine regions in Germany, both famous for Riesling. The Pfalz is warmer and often gives fuller, riper styles. Neither is in Austria, France or Switzerland.

Riesling is rarely aged in new oak because winemakers want to keep its pure fruit and floral aromas. Vanilla and toast from new barrels would cover them up, so most Riesling is made in stainless steel or old, neutral casks. Riesling is not too low in acid for oak, oak is not banned for whites, and oak has nothing to do with bubbles.

German labels carry useful words. Trocken means dry, and Halbtrocken means off-dry. Germany''s top category is Prädikatswein, a quality category graded by how ripe the grapes were at harvest. It is not a basic table wine or a sparkling wine, and Landwein, not Prädikatswein, is Germany''s PGI level.

The Prädikat levels rise with ripeness. Kabinett is the lightest. Spätlese means late harvest: the grapes are picked later and riper than Kabinett. A Spätlese can be dry or sweet, so check for Trocken. Auslese is riper again, then come the sweet Beerenauslese and Trockenbeerenauslese. Trockenbeerenauslese, or TBA, is made from the ripest grapes, shrivelled and usually affected by noble rot. Despite the word trocken in its name, TBA is intensely sweet; here the word refers to the dried berries.

Riesling also shines in Australia. The Clare Valley and Eden Valley in South Australia are best known for dry Riesling. These wines have intense lime and citrus flavours and can develop toasty notes with age. Shiraz grows nearby, but Riesling is the grape these valleys are known for.

Good Riesling ages well. Over the years it can gain honey and toast, and its acidity keeps it fresh for decades.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-aromatic-whites', 'Sauvignon Blanc, Chenin Blanc and the Alsace whites', 'Region', ARRAY['aroma', 'acidity', 'sweetness', 'body']::text[], 'Several white grapes are worth knowing by smell and style. The Loire Valley in France and Alsace, on the German border, are good places to meet them.

Sauvignon Blanc is crisp, dry and aromatic, with high acidity and green notes such as grass, gooseberry and green pepper. In the eastern Loire it makes Sancerre and its neighbour across the river, Pouilly-Fumé. Pouilly-Fumé, from the Loire Valley, is made from Sauvignon Blanc, in a dry, crisp style. It is not Chardonnay, Chenin Blanc or Riesling, even though "fumé" suggests smoke.

New Zealand turned the grape into a global hit. A Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc typically shows pronounced green and tropical aromas, such as gooseberry and passion fruit, with high acidity. The region is sunny but cool, which gives pungent aromas while keeping the acid crisp. It is usually unoaked, so expect no buttery oak, no red fruit and no nutty, aged flavours.

Further west in the Loire lies Vouvray. Vouvray is made from Chenin Blanc. Chenin has naturally high acidity and flavours of apple, quince and honey, and Vouvray comes in every style from dry to sweet, plus sparkling. It is not Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay or Viognier.

Alsace makes rich, aromatic whites, usually labelled by grape. Pinot Gris and Pinot Grigio are the same grape. Compared with a typical Italian Pinot Grigio, an Alsace Pinot Gris is usually fuller-bodied and riper, sometimes off-dry. Italian versions are mostly light, crisp and neutral; Alsace gives stone fruit, spice and weight. Pinot Gris makes white wine, and it is not normally sparkling.

Gewurztraminer is the most perfumed of all. Alsace Gewurztraminer is known for aromas of lychee, rose and sweet spice. It is usually deep in colour, full-bodied and fairly low in acidity, sometimes with a touch of sweetness. Green pepper and grass belong to Sauvignon Blanc, blackcurrant and cedar to Cabernet Sauvignon, and bread and biscuit to Champagne.

A quick rule: if a white smells of cut grass and gooseberry, think Sauvignon Blanc; if it smells of lychee and roses, think Gewurztraminer.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-rhone-syrah', 'The Rhône Valley: Syrah, Grenache and Viognier', 'Region', ARRAY['aroma', 'body', 'alcohol', 'tannin']::text[], 'The Rhône Valley, in south-east France, splits into two halves with different grapes. Learn which grape leads in each half and most Rhône labels make sense.

The Northern Rhône is a narrow strip of steep, terraced slopes. Here red wines are based on Syrah. Côte Rôtie and Hermitage are Northern Rhône wines made mainly from Syrah, as is the larger and more affordable Crozes-Hermitage. Northern Rhône Syrah is deep in colour with blackberry, black pepper, smoked meat and olive notes. These wines are not Grenache, Merlot or Pinot Noir.

The Northern Rhône also has a famous white. Condrieu is made from Viognier, an aromatic white grape with peach, apricot and floral aromas. Viognier gives a fuller body and fairly low acidity, quite unlike lean Chardonnay, Riesling or Albariño.

The Southern Rhône is wider, flatter and hotter. Grenache, which loves heat, is the main grape here. The main variety in most red Châteauneuf-du-Pape is Grenache. Southern Rhône reds, including Côtes du Rhône, are usually Grenache-led blends, often with Syrah and Mourvèdre. Expect ripe red fruit such as strawberry and raspberry, high alcohol and spice. Syrah plays a supporting role in the south, and Pinot Noir and Cabernet Sauvignon are not Rhône grapes.

Syrah travels widely, and in Australia it goes by the name Shiraz. Australia''s Barossa Valley is famous for full-bodied, ripe wines from Shiraz. The Barossa is warm, so the grapes get very ripe, giving rich, high-alcohol wine with ripe black fruit and often sweet spice from oak. The region''s fame rests on Shiraz, not Pinot Noir, Riesling or Sauvignon Blanc.

Here the grape name tells you something about climate. Syrah and Shiraz are the same grape. Compared with a Barossa Valley Shiraz, a Northern Rhône Syrah such as Crozes-Hermitage is often more peppery and savoury, and less jammy. The more moderate Northern Rhône keeps black pepper and savoury notes, while the warm Barossa gives riper, jammier fruit. Both are deep red, and both are far fuller than a rosé.

Producers in cooler parts of the New World sometimes write Syrah on the label to signal the leaner Rhône style, while Shiraz hints at the riper Australian style.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-spain', 'Spain: Tempranillo, Rioja and Albariño', 'Region', ARRAY['label', 'quality', 'acidity']::text[], 'Spain has more vineyard land than almost any other country, and a few grapes and label terms unlock most of its wines.

Tempranillo is Spain''s great red grape. It gives red fruit such as strawberry and cherry, medium to high tannin and moderate acidity, and it takes well to oak, which adds vanilla, coconut and, with age, leather notes. Ribera del Duero, on a high plateau in northern Spain, is known for red wines from Tempranillo. It is the key grape there, just as it is in Rioja. Garnacha, the Spanish name for Grenache, is grown widely too and often blended in Rioja, but Ribera del Duero is not known for Garnacha, Malbec or Merlot.

Rioja is Spain''s most famous red. It holds the DOCa category, Spain''s top level, above DO. Rioja labels are known for ageing terms, which tell you how long the wine spent in barrel and bottle before release. In order of increasing age they are Joven, Crianza, Reserva and Gran Reserva. Joven means young, with little or no oak. Crianza has spent some time in oak. Reserva has been aged for longer. On a Rioja label, Gran Reserva shows the longest ageing before release, with the longest minimum time in barrel and bottle. Long ageing gives softer tannin and flavours of dried fruit, vanilla and leather.

Spain''s best-known white comes from the green, rainy north-west. Rías Baixas, in Galicia, is cool and wet, close to the Atlantic. Rías Baixas is known for the white grape Albariño. Albariño makes dry, fresh wine with high acidity and citrus and peach fruit, ideal with seafood. Furmint belongs to Hungary, Garganega to Italy''s Soave and Chenin Blanc to the Loire, so none of them is the Rías Baixas grape.

Spain also makes Cava, a traditional-method sparkling wine, and Sherry, a fortified wine from the south. Both are covered in their own articles.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-new-world', 'New World regions: Chile, Argentina, South Africa and Australia', 'Region', ARRAY['cabernet', 'merlot', 'label']::text[], 'Outside Europe, wine labels usually name the grape, so the useful skill is linking each country to its signature regions and varieties.

Chile is a long, thin country between the Andes and the Pacific. Its Central Valley, a large source of fruity Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon, is in Chile. It is warm and sunny, so it produces large volumes of ripe, fruity reds at good value. It is not in Argentina, Australia or South Africa. Chile also adopted a grape that almost vanished from Bordeaux. Carmenère is especially associated with Chile, where it is now grown mainly in the Central Valley. It gives deep-coloured reds with black fruit and green, herbal notes. Its old home is France, but today it belongs to Chile, not Italy or New Zealand.

Across the Andes lies Argentina. Malbec is the signature grape of Mendoza, Argentina. Mendoza''s vineyards are high and sunny, and the altitude keeps nights cool. Malbec there gives deep colour, ripe black fruit such as plum and blackberry, and soft tannin. Rioja, the Barossa and Marlborough are known for other grapes.

South Africa''s wine heartland is near Cape Town. Stellenbosch is a wine region in South Africa, one of its best known, especially for Cabernet Sauvignon and red blends. It is not in Chile, New Zealand or Australia. South Africa also has a grape of its own. Pinotage is a black grape most associated with South Africa, where it was bred by crossing Pinot Noir with Cinsault. A Cape Blend is a red blend that includes Pinotage.

Australia is famous for warm-climate Shiraz, but its regions vary. Coonawarra in South Australia is especially known for Cabernet Sauvignon. Its red terra rossa soil over limestone and its moderate climate give Cabernet with blackcurrant fruit, firm tannin and often a minty edge. Pinot Noir and Riesling do better in other, cooler parts of Australia, such as Tasmania or the Clare and Eden Valleys.

A pattern runs through all these places. Warm, sunny regions give riper fruit, softer acidity and fuller body. Growers seek out cooler sites, through altitude, ocean breezes or latitude, when they want fresher styles.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-sparkling', 'How sparkling wine gets its bubbles', 'Term', ARRAY['sparkling', 'champagne', 'sweetness', 'label']::text[], 'Bubbles come from carbon dioxide made during fermentation. Most sparkling wines start as a still base wine, then go through a second fermentation in a sealed container that traps the gas. Where that happens is the key difference between styles.

Champagne, from northern France, uses the traditional method. The three main grapes used for Champagne are Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Meunier. Chardonnay is white; Pinot Noir and Meunier are black, but pressing them gently and quickly gives clear juice, so they can still make white wine.

In Champagne, the second fermentation takes place in the bottle the wine is sold in. Yeast and a little sugar are added to the still wine, the bottle is sealed, and the gas has nowhere to go. It does not happen in a tank, in open barrels or in the vineyard.

The dead yeast, called lees, stays in the bottle afterwards. The bread and biscuit flavours in Champagne come from long ageing on the lees after the second fermentation. Over months or years the yeast breaks down, giving bread, biscuit and toast notes. No oak chips, spirit or tropical grapes are involved.

Other countries use the same method. Cava, from Spain, is made by the traditional method, with a second fermentation in bottle, mostly from Spanish grapes. On a South African label, "Cap Classique" means a sparkling wine made by the traditional method. Neither is fortified, and Cap Classique has nothing to do with Pinotage or old vines.

Labels also show sweetness. Brut means dry, and it is the most common term. Demi-Sec is noticeably sweeter.

The tank method is quicker and cheaper. For most Prosecco, the second fermentation takes place in a large sealed tank, and the wine is bottled under pressure. This keeps fresh, fruity grape aromas rather than yeasty ones. Prosecco comes from north-east Italy and is made mainly from Glera, which gives light aromas of pear, apple and flowers. It is not Chardonnay, Pinot Noir or Moscato.

Moscato is the grape behind Asti, from Piedmont. Asti is a sweet, low-alcohol sparkling wine with grapey aromas. It is made by the tank method, and fermentation stops early, leaving sugar and little alcohol. It is not dry, long-aged or fortified.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;
INSERT INTO articles (slug, title, category, topics, content) VALUES ('l2-fortified', 'Fortified wines: Port and Sherry', 'Term', ARRAY['sweetness', 'alcohol', 'service']::text[], 'A fortified wine has grape spirit added to it, which raises the alcohol to roughly 15 to 22 per cent. The two classics, Port and Sherry, differ mainly in when that spirit goes in.

Port comes from the Douro Valley in northern Portugal and is almost always red and sweet. Port is naturally sweet because grape spirit is added during fermentation, which stops the yeast before all the sugar is used. The spirit lifts the alcohol so high that the yeast can no longer work, and the unfermented grape sugar stays in the wine. No sugar syrup is added, the grapes are not frozen, and Port is not aged under flor.

Port comes in several styles. Ruby Port is young and fruity, aged briefly before bottling. Reserve Ruby is a better-quality ruby. Late Bottled Vintage, or LBV, comes from a single year but spends four to six years in wood, so it is ready to drink on release. Vintage Port is the style made from a single, excellent year and meant to age for many years in bottle. Producers declare a vintage only in the best years. The wine spends only a short time in barrel and is bottled young, so it develops slowly. It throws a heavy sediment, which is why Vintage Port usually needs decanting. Tawny Port, by contrast, ages for years in barrel and turns brown and nutty.

Sherry comes from around Jerez in southern Spain and is made mostly from the white Palomino grape. Unlike Port, Sherry is fermented to dryness first, and the spirit is added afterwards. So all Sherry starts dry; sweet styles are sweetened later.

Fino Sherry ages under a layer of yeast called flor. This film floats on the surface of the wine in the barrel. It protects the wine from oxygen and gives a pale, dry Sherry with bready, tangy, almond notes. Flor is not the same as lees, which are dead yeast that sinks to the bottom, nor botrytis, the noble rot fungus, nor must, which is unfermented grape juice.

Serve Fino chilled and fresh, with salty snacks, olives or seafood. Serve Port at room temperature or slightly cool, with blue cheese or chocolate.') ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET title = EXCLUDED.title, category = EXCLUDED.category, topics = EXCLUDED.topics, content = EXCLUDED.content;

